    "body": "Great on any Friday: Anker 535 Fast Car Charger - 67W, 2x USB-C + 1x USB-A, PowerIQ 3.0, 3ft Cable Included\nTesla Cybertruck Gets $5,000 More Expensive After the Latest Price Hike, and It Makes No Sense\n66 photos\nPhoto: Tesla\nTesla recently announced that the Cybertruck production would stop next summer. This apparently caused a spike in interest toward the weird pickup truck, because Tesla repeatedly raised its price. The latest round saw another $5,000 added to the price of the Cyberbeast, the only variant that is still available to order. This puts the entry price at $114,990, closer to the Foundation Series price but without the perks.\nTesla Cybertruck has always been a slow-selling model after the initial hype evaporated in the first half of 2024. Since then, Tesla could barely sell around 6-7,000 units per quarter, one-tenth of what Tesla thought was possible. Considering the high development costs that aren't shared with any other model in Tesla's lineup, this suggests that the Cybertruck program was losing money.\nUnsurprisingly, Tesla decided to cut its losses and announced that the Cybertruck would be retired next year. The move, although highly sensible and also anticipated, left a lot of people speechless. Even Tesla's biggest fans could not get over the fact that the Cybertruck was the third model Tesla axed over the past year, with the Model S and Model X also retired earlier this year.\nThe Cybertruck's appeal is boosted by FOMO Although the Cybertruck was undoubtedly canceled because it was losing money, Elon Musk vigorously denied it, saying that the Cybertruck was still profitable (God knows how). If that was the case, I fail to comprehend why Tesla would cut such a money maker.\nBesides that, Elon Musk is known for having a complicated relationship with the truth. The best example is denying that Tesla's Gen-3 compact EV was scrapped in 2024, even though he knew very well that the story was true .\nThe funny thing is that a lot of people just realized they wanted a Cybertruck only after it received its last communion from Patriarch Musk. Tesla is usually opaque with its sales numbers, but on the used car market, Cybertruck's prices spiked to new heights, suggesting increased interest.\nPhoto: Tesla A recent iSeeCars study even placed the Cybertruck as the fastest-selling used truck in the US, spending an average of only 39 days on the market. However, the same is also true for other electric pickups, including the Rivian R1T and the Ford F-150 Lightning. In fact, 16 of the 25 fastest-selling used cars in the US are fully electric, reflecting a new trend in the market.\nTesla keeps raising the Cybertruck's prices Tesla wants us to think that the new Cybertrucks are just as hot as the used ones, because it keeps bumping the prices after discontinuing the cheaper variants. It had already increased the price when Musk announced its demise earlier this month, with the Cyberbeast starting at $102,490 (+$2,500). Just three days later, Tesla added another $7,500 to the sticker.\nBarely two weeks have passed, and Tesla already wants $5,000 more. Obviously, it wants to convey the message that the Cybertruck is the hottest car on the lot. If this is true, the Tesla Cybertruck went from being the Cinderella of Tesla's lineup to, well, still being Cinderella, but after the prince found out that she was the one with the missing shoe.\nForgive me for not buying Cybertruck's sudden popularity. Tesla fans were just as proud to proclaim that the demand for the weirdly-shaped electric pickup was \"off the charts\" over the summer, when Tesla offered the base AWD variant at $59,990 . However, when the delivery numbers were published, Cybertruck's sales were barely visible in the \"Other models\" category. I suspect this will happen again when Q4 2026 production and delivery numbers are released in January.\nYou can't lose money on a truck you don't make If the Cybertruck is really that successful at $115k, why is Tesla stopping production? After all, Musk said that the electric pickup program was not losing money when the Cybertruck retailed for $99,990. With $25k added as a bonus, it should make enough money to be one of the most profitable models in Tesla's lineup.\nPhoto: Tesla And if it's not actually selling that well, which I believe is more likely, why does Tesla keep raising the price? I'm more inclined to believe that Musk's company is losing a lot of money, and raising the price is just one way of compensating for these losses. After all, there's no economy of scale due to Cybertruck's abysmal sales.\nRaising prices is also a way of pushing the potential buyers away. In this case, Tesla signals that it doesn't really want to build more Cybertrucks. You can't lose money on a truck you don't make. And if enough people consider it too expensive, it might just offer Musk another reason to disregard those who asked him to reconsider the Cybertruck's fate.\nAmong them is one of Tesla's most successful partners, Unplugged Performance. The performance part supplier has asked Tesla publicly to let it build Cybertrucks on its own money. Even better, Unplugged Performance offered to buy the production line and pay Tesla a license fee for every Cybertruck it builds. It also wants to expand the lineup with the famous CyberSUV that everyone wanted and Tesla refused to make.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nTesla Cybertruck Tesla price hike fomo\nAbout the author: Cristian Agatie\nAfter his childhood dream of becoming a \"tractor operator\" didn't pan out, Cristian turned to journalism, first in print and later moving to online media. His top interests are electric vehicles and new energy solutions. Full profile\nUP NEXT NASA Needs a Nuclear Reactor for the Moon, Now Looking for Someone to Build It",
